      {
        "h": "Delirium — causes and nursing role",
        "p": "Delirium is an acute confusional state and a medical emergency, often the first sign of illness (infection, hypoxia, dehydration, pain, urinary retention, constipation, drugs, metabolic upset). The nurse recognises it early with the CAM, searches for and treats the cause, ensures hydration/oxygenation, provides orientation and a calm familiar environment, and minimises restraints and psychoactive drugs. It is superimposed on dementia in many hospitalised elders."
      },
      {
        "h": "4. Frailty",
        "p": "Frailty is a state of increased vulnerability from cumulative decline across systems, so that a minor stressor causes disproportionate decline. The Fried phenotype defines it by three or more of: unintentional weight loss, weakness (weak grip), exhaustion, slow walking speed, and low physical activity. Nursing: resistance exercise, protein-adequate nutrition, medication review, and preventing hospital-acquired decline."
      },
      {
        "h": "5. Polypharmacy",
        "p": "Polypharmacy is the use of multiple medications (commonly defined as five or more), which raises the risk of adverse drug reactions, interactions, non-adherence, falls and delirium. Nursing: maintain an accurate medication list, reconcile at every transition, watch for 'prescribing cascades', simplify regimens, and flag potentially inappropriate drugs for review."
      },
      {
        "h": "6. Pressure injury",
        "list": [
          "Localised damage to skin and underlying tissue over a bony prominence from sustained pressure, shear and friction.",
          "Risk rises with immobility, incontinence, malnutrition, poor perfusion and thin ageing skin; screen with the Braden Scale.",
          "Prevention (nursing): reposition every ~2 hours, pressure-redistributing surfaces, keep skin clean and dry, manage moisture, optimise nutrition and hydration, inspect skin daily."
        ]
      },
      {
        "h": "7. Malnutrition",
        "list": [
          "Common and under-recognised; causes include poor dentition, reduced appetite, depression, poverty, dysphagia, dementia and chronic disease.",
          "Screen with the Mini Nutritional Assessment (MNA); monitor weight and intake.",
          "Nursing: appetising, easy-to-eat, nutrient- and protein-dense meals; assistance with feeding; treat oral/dental problems; manage dysphagia; supplements when indicated."
        ]
      },
      {
        "h": "8. Sensory impairment",
        "p": "Vision and hearing loss are highly prevalent and drive isolation, depression, falls, delirium and functional decline, yet are often correctable. Nursing: ensure glasses and hearing aids are worn and functioning, optimise lighting and reduce glare, communicate face-to-face in a low pitch, and refer for cataract surgery or audiology."
      },
      {
        "h": "Clinical relevance",
        "p": "Geriatric syndromes cluster, share risk factors and amplify one another — immobility causes pressure injury and incontinence; incontinence and sedatives cause falls; infection causes delirium. Because each is multifactorial, the nurse must assess and intervene across several domains at once rather than treating a single 'cause'."
      }
